Element			Type		Purpose
--------------------------------------------------
<BrowserRouter>		Component	Wraps the entire application to synchronize your UI with the browser's HTML5 History API.
<Routes> & <Route>	Components	Works as a matching container. <Route> maps a specific path (e.g., /about) to a React element.
<Link> & <NavLink>	Components	Replaces traditional <a> tags. It prevents page reloads and can dynamically style itself if active.
useNavigate()		Hook		Allows you to redirect users programmatically via code .
useParams()		Hook		Extracts dynamic URL variables (like retrieving the numeric id from /user/42).


steps for routing in react
---------------------------
1. install react-router-dom libraries
	npm install react-router-dom
2. configure the route paths for your components in App.jsx
3. to do step 2 we need (BrowserRouter>, <Routes>, <Route>)
4. test the routes in the browser
5. but this doesn't help, we have to dynamically change the url
6. this can be done in 2 ways
	a. use <Link> component
	b. programmatically navigate using useNavigate() hook
		a. Use <Link> component
			- create a <Header> component
			- use <Link> components instead of <a>
			- include <Header> component inside <BrowserRouter> in App.jsx 
		b. programmatically navigate using useNavigate() hook
			- import { useNavigate } from "react-router-dom";
			- const navigate = useNavigate();
			- navigate("/fruitlist");

7. Nested routes
	- configure the nested child routes in App.jsx
	- create a kind of a dashboard component with a nav header and use <Outlet> component
8. Pass Route parameters
	- define the route path with route param as
		<Route path="/dash/movies/edit/:movId" element={<MovieEdit />} />
	- to retrieve the route param use the useParams hook in the component

Steps to attach login component
-------------------------------
1. Create the Login component, and capture login form data
2. create userService.js to consume endpoints for login and register
3. create authService.js to do auth related tasks such as storeJwt, retrieveJwt, removeJwt, storeUser, getUser, isLoggedIn, isAdmin, isCustomer
4. upon successful login
	- store the userInfo using authService
	- store the jwt token using authService
	- navigate to /dash
5. in dashboard component conditionally render the Links based on role of the user
6. create logout link and navigate to login component
7. in login component, when the component mounts for the first time clear session storage
